WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.507
A practical Bayesian network workflow begins with a clear reasoning question.

2
00:00:05.507 --> 00:00:14.018
Are you supporting equipment diagnosis, weather-related risk, fraud review, security alert triage, project risk, or system diagnostics?

3
00:00:14.018 --> 00:00:16.021
The question defines scope.

4
00:00:16.021 --> 00:00:25.534
Without scope, it is easy to add variables because they are interesting rather than because they support a decision.

5
00:00:25.534 --> 00:00:28.037
Next, identify relevant uncertain variables.

6
00:00:28.037 --> 00:00:30.040
Represent them as nodes.

7
00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:32.543
Draw an acyclic dependency graph.

8
00:00:32.543 --> 00:00:35.547
Define parents, children, ancestors, and descendants.

9
00:00:35.547 --> 00:00:47.563
Check conditional independence assumptions: does each variable depend directly on the variables you chose as parents, and not on unrelated non-descendants once those parents

10
00:00:47.563 --> 00:00:48.564
are known?

11
00:00:48.564 --> 00:00:53.070
The graph should make assumptions visible enough for review.

12
00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:55.573
Then define local probability distributions.

13
00:00:55.573 --> 00:00:57.576
Root nodes receive priors.

14
00:00:57.576 --> 00:01:02.582
Child nodes receive conditional probability tables or other conditional distributions.

15
00:01:02.582 --> 00:01:08.090
The graph allows the joint distribution to factor into local terms.

16
00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:15.599
Evidence can then be entered, queries can be asked, and posterior probabilities can be computed.

17
00:01:15.599 --> 00:01:27.115
The model may support predictive reasoning from causes to effects, diagnostic reasoning from effects to causes, and intercausal reasoning such as explaining away.

18
00:01:27.115 --> 00:01:30.119
Finally, validate and maintain the model.

19
00:01:30.119 --> 00:01:31.120
Test scenarios.

20
00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:32.121
Check calibration.

21
00:01:32.121 --> 00:01:33.623
Run sensitivity analysis.

22
00:01:33.623 --> 00:01:39.631
Document assumptions, probability sources, missing-data behavior, limitations, versions, owners, and review criteria.

23
00:01:39.631 --> 00:01:44.638
Monitor for model drift and revise when the environment changes.

24
00:01:44.638 --> 00:01:48.142
A Bayesian network does not eliminate uncertainty.

25
00:01:48.142 --> 00:01:59.157
Its value is that it makes uncertain assumptions and relationships explicit, so they can be reviewed, updated, tested, and used more carefully.

26
00:01:59.157 --> 00:02:11.173
The course has covered the major pieces: nodes, edges, priors, conditional probabilities, CPTs, factorization, evidence, inference, posterior updating, predictive reasoning, diagnostic reasoning, intercausal reasoning,

27
00:02:11.173 --> 00:02:16.179
explaining away, parameter learning, structure learning, validation, and responsible use.

28
00:02:16.179 --> 00:02:18.182
Keep the workflow practical.

29
00:02:18.182 --> 00:02:30.198
Start with the decision, model only what is needed, document uncertainty honestly, and revise the network when evidence shows the assumptions are no longer

30
00:02:30.198 --> 00:02:31.199
good enough.
